# Two jobs here, and they pull in opposite directions.
#
# 1. A user's financial data must never be committed, whatever they name it.
# 2. The engine's own source and the invented example must always be committed.
#
# The data patterns below are ANCHORED AT THE REPO ROOT (a leading slash) rather
# than written as `**/statements/`. The wildcard form once excluded
# `lib/closethebooks/statements/`, which is the Python that parses bank
# statements, so a fresh clone was missing the parser package while every local
# test passed, because the files were already on disk. A gitignore negation
# cannot rescue a file inside an excluded directory, so the rule itself has to
# be narrow. Anchoring is enough: git only ignores paths inside this repo, and a
# working directory the user keeps elsewhere is not this repo's concern.

# A profile holds one real company's chart, vendors and open questions.
# The engine ships; the company does not.
profiles/*.local.json
profiles/*.local.yaml

# A working directory: exports, statements, review workbooks, import files,
# answers. All of it is somebody's financial data.
/workdir/
/exports/
/statements/
/for-review/
/review/
/import/
/answers/
/questions/
/reports/
/handoff/
**/workdir/
*.APPROVED

# Real financial documents, wherever they land, except the invented company's.
*.qbo
*.qbx
*.qba
*.ofx
/*.pdf
/*.xlsx
/*.csv

__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.venv/
venv/
.DS_Store
build/
dist/
*.egg-info/
